                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_qualify:       `<strong>That's the disciplined move.</strong> You qualified the brief instead of accepting it whole. Helvetia's transformation strategy and competency model are genuinely theirs; most of the leadership content you already own. Now you can design where customisation actually earns its keep.`,
                    coach_r1_fullbespoke:   `<strong>You've just agreed to five custom builds for 600 people at $7,500 a head.</strong> That is exactly the flagship design that delivered a 9% margin last year — delighted clients, money lost, faculty burned. Before you design anything: what in this brief is genuinely Helvetia-specific, and what is generic content you already own?`,
                    coach_r1_standardise:   `<strong>Smart on margin — one global course, maximum reuse.</strong> But Helvetia's CHRO asked for it to feel local in every region, and your design is identical from São Paulo to Singapore. NPS will fall and a region will escalate to the Group. Where do you spend a little bespoke — and is that the configured layer rather than full custom?`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp qualification.</strong> You separated client-specific from generic AND named the real regional differences over a common core. That is the lens that lets you build one journey that genuinely feels local in five places without five custom builds.`,
                    coach_r1_regionall:     `<strong>Treating every regional ask as real is how the budget explodes.</strong> Some of it is genuine — language, regulation, local cases — but a lot is tailoring habit. Five distinct designs cannot fit $4.5M or the faculty bench. Find the common core first.`,
                    coach_r1_regionnone:    `<strong>Calling all regional difference noise is the over-standardise trap.</strong> Language, regulation and local market cases are real. An identical global design tanks NPS and hands the Middle East an excuse to escalate. Keep a common core, but allow a thin real local layer.`,
                    coach_r1_lowfund:       `<strong>You barely tested the economics.</strong> The dean's 25% floor is non-negotiable this year, and at $7,500 a head the brief is tight. Qualify what's fundable and renegotiate the gaps now — not after you've over-promised.`,
                    coach_r2_coreplus:      `<strong>Platform-and-modules done right.</strong> A strong common core plus a thin, high-impact configured layer per region delivers perceived customisation at near-standardised cost. That is the structural answer to scale vs bespoke.`,
                    coach_r2_thincore:      `<strong>A thin core with thick regional builds is five programmes wearing a trench coat.</strong> It feels unique but it blows the budget and the bench — and it loses the consistency that lets you run it across continents. Thicken the core, thin the local layer.`,
                    coach_r2_onlycore:      `<strong>Core-only is the generic-course trap.</strong> Identical everywhere means Helvetia paid a premium for an open-enrolment programme. A thin configured layer per region is cheap insurance for NPS — add it.`,
                    coach_r2_middle:        `<strong>You found the money in the middle.</strong> Configured modules — your existing content rebuilt around Helvetia's own transformation cases — capture most of the perceived-customisation value at a fraction of the faculty-days of full bespoke. That's the high-leverage zone.`,
                    coach_r2_nomiddle:      `<strong>You've sorted everything into reuse or build-from-scratch.</strong> The standard deck feels generic and the bespoke build blows the budget. The money is in the middle: take your change-leadership module and rebuild the cases around Helvetia's transformation — same faculty-days, far higher fit. How many modules could be configured instead of built?`,
                    coach_r2_allshelf:      `<strong>Almost all off-the-shelf reads as generic.</strong> You protected margin and capacity, but Helvetia can buy this open-enrolment. Move a chunk into the configured middle so it carries their cases and data without a full custom build.`,
                    coach_r2_concentrated:  `<strong>Bespoke where it differentiates.</strong> Concentrating your scarce custom build on the modules that carry Helvetia's transformation strategy is exactly right — that's where bespoke earns its cost; everywhere else, configure and reuse.`,
                    coach_r2_spread:        `<strong>Spreading bespoke thinly dilutes it.</strong> A little custom on every module costs nearly as much as concentrating it and differentiates nothing. Pick the one or two modules that matter and go deep there; configure the rest.`,
                    coach_r2_genericbespoke:`<strong>Custom-building the fundamentals is spend with no payoff.</strong> Generic leadership content is exactly what should be off-the-shelf or configured. Save bespoke for what is genuinely Helvetia-specific.`,
                    coach_r3_pilot:         `<strong>Pilot-then-scale de-risks the rollout.</strong> Launch one region, learn, then roll the proven design to the other four. If the design has a flaw, you fix it once — not 600 times across five time zones.`,
                    coach_r3_parallel:      `<strong>Five parallel launches in Month 1 is high-wire.</strong> If the design has a flaw, you've now made it 600 times across five time zones with no chance to fix it. A pilot region costs a month and de-risks the other four. What does pilot-then-scale save you in rework?`,
                    coach_r3_staggered:     `<strong>Staggered pairs is a reasonable middle path.</strong> You get some learning between waves without a single slow pilot. Just make sure the first wave genuinely informs the next — staggering without learning is just slower parallel.`,
                    coach_r3_balanced:      `<strong>A sustainable staffing model.</strong> Marquee faculty on the differentiating modules, regional faculty for credible local delivery, and a train-the-facilitator cohort to extend the bench. You please Helvetia without burning the professors you need for the other 54 engagements.`,
                    coach_r3_overbook:      `<strong>You've over-booked the marquee bench.</strong> To hit the quality Helvetia wants you've scheduled your top professors past safe utilisation. They're already above 80% and they've warned they'll step back. Win this and you lose the faculty you need elsewhere. Who teaches the configured modules — and where's your train-the-facilitator plan?`,
                    coach_r3_thinbench:     `<strong>Your bench is too thin.</strong> Leaning almost entirely on marquee and regional faculty with little train-the-facilitator leaves no durable local capability — and pushes utilisation up. Invest in extending the bench so the model survives the rollout.`,
                    coach_r3_nomarquee:     `<strong>Too few marquee days starves the differentiators.</strong> The modules that carry Helvetia's strategy need credible senior faculty. Stripping marquee to zero protects utilisation but hollows out the perceived quality you're selling.`,
                    coach_r3_noreuse:       `<strong>You left the reuse savings on the table.</strong> The margin bridge depends on reusing configured modules across all five regions — design once, deliver five times. Price that reuse in or you won't clear the 25% floor.`,
                    coach_r4_balanced:      `<strong>A scorecard the dean would sign.</strong> Defensible targets across NPS, margin, faculty utilisation and on-time delivery — you've resolved the trade-offs instead of pretending they don't exist. That is a renewable engagement.`,
                    coach_r4_maxnps:        `<strong>You maxed NPS and recreated last year's loss.</strong> Beautiful client satisfaction, but margin is gone and your faculty are at 95% — a happier client and the same money-losing flagship. The renewal needs all four. Which number did you sacrifice, and would the dean sign this?`,
                    coach_r4_maxmargin:     `<strong>Margin-safe, but you've built a generic programme.</strong> You protected the floor by stripping the experience to the bone — and NPS fell. Helvetia feels it bought an open-enrolment course and the three-year academy goes to a rival. Balance, don't maximise one.`,
                    coach_r4_proof:         `<strong>That's a renewal-grade pitch.</strong> You showed Year 1 is simultaneously delightful, profitable, deliverable and sustainable on the bench — the four-way case that earns the $13M academy rather than just a happy first cohort.`,
                    coach_r4_crowdpleaser:  `<strong>Promising even more customisation is how you lose money faster.</strong> It locks the client in emotionally and locks you into the flagship trap. Renewal comes from proving the design is sustainable, not from escalating the bespoke promise.`,
                    coach_r4_discount:      `<strong>Winning renewal on price guts the margin you just protected.</strong> A discount on a $13M commitment trades away the floor the dean made non-negotiable. Win on the balanced value of the design, not on price.`,
                    coach_r4_decline:       `<strong>Declining the $1M is the disciplined answer.</strong> The extra cash can't buy faculty capacity you don't have, and full bespoke across five regions breaks sustainable design. Protecting the model protects the renewal — the dean signs this.`,
                    coach_r4_accept:        `<strong>Taking the $1M for full bespoke stretches a bench that's already warning you.</strong> The revenue looks good until two marquee professors step back and you can't staff the other engagements. More money can't manufacture faculty-days.`,
                    coach_r4_partial:       `<strong>Channelling extra budget into the configured layer is defensible.</strong> It deepens perceived customisation without the faculty-day blowout of full bespoke — better than refusing the conversation, though declining outright best protects the bench.`,

